 This can be done in parallel with the concept called self-attention which can capture extremely

72
00:06:45,080 --> 00:06:51,880
 powerful dependencies between different symbols appeared in the input data and then you can

73
00:06:51,880 --> 00:06:55,000
 basically do all sorts of things.

74
00:06:55,000 --> 00:07:01,920
 So the generative BI for example the chat GPT and other things essentially relying on

75
00:07:01,920 --> 00:07:04,320
 the transformer model.

76
00:07:04,320 --> 00:07:12,480
 In all of these cases we will see how these architectures can be changed or modified to

77
00:07:12,480 --> 00:07:17,920
 achieve the transfer learning.

78
00:07:17,920 --> 00:07:24,360
 So the essence of deep learning is basically representation learning.

79
00:07:24,360 --> 00:07:27,640
 So that's the power main power of deep learning.

80
00:07:27,640 --> 00:07:36,080
 So the input data could be like image, video, text or audio and then you have this model

81
00:07:36,080 --> 00:07:42,600
 involving a billion sometimes millions and billions of parameters and they will produce

82
00:07:42,600 --> 00:07:49,260
 a hidden vector of some dimension fixed dimension typically the numerical the real number vectors

83
00:07:49,260 --> 00:07:55,840
 and these vectors will finally help us to produce the final output.

84
00:07:55,840 --> 00:08:02,440
 Now the vectors that we get from these models is not human understandable in the sense that

85
00:08:02,440 --> 00:08:07,040
 if we try we cannot really make sense out of it.

86
00:08:07,040 --> 00:08:14,360
 So all the machines do they know that how this can be used different models the computational

87
00:08:14,360 --> 00:08:20,000
 models they understand how this can be used to achieve the final goal.

88
00:08:20,000 --> 00:08:25,400
 So to give you a little bit of sense of these vectors created by the deep learning models.

89
00:08:25,400 --> 00:08:30,120
 So ultimately what they do they follow discriminative sort of the learning paradigm.

90
00:08:30,120 --> 00:08:35,660
 So if two objects are very different to each other then the vectors produced by the deep

91
00:08:35,660 --> 00:08:40,360
 learning models will be very different so angle between them will be very high or distance

92
00:08:40,360 --> 00:08:45,040
 between them will be very high or something like that.

93
00:08:45,040 --> 00:08:54,120
 So the typical deep learning model as I mentioned will involve a millions and billions of parameters

94
00:08:54,120 --> 00:09:00,520
 you know and these parameters typically are organized in a particular way so that you

95
00:09:00,520 --> 00:09:05,520
 can do the lot of feature engineering and so on and so forth.

96
00:09:05,520 --> 00:09:11,280
 So whenever I talk about the parameters I will denote by this set of parameters W typically

97
00:09:11,280 --> 00:09:21,400
 the weights and B for the biases.

98
00:09:21,400 --> 00:09:27,040
 So typically these two sets of parameters that you have in the deep learning models will

99
00:09:27,040 --> 00:09:29,840
 determine everything for you.

100
00:09:29,840 --> 00:09:35,800
 So general belief is that higher the number of parameters better capability that they

101
00:09:35,800 --> 00:09:42,960
 have but the downside is that higher the model more computational power will be required

102
00:09:42,960 --> 00:09:51,120
 more data will be required and that is where transfer learning comes into picture.

103
00:09:51,120 --> 00:09:58,800
 The first question that we encounter in transfer learning why transfer learning.

104
00:09:58,800 --> 00:10:06,440
 So the first assumption on which transfer learning stands is that there are many tasks

105
00:10:06,440 --> 00:10:10,200
 that we do often they share common knowledge.

106
00:10:10,200 --> 00:10:19,120
 For example the linguistic representations of text fragments will share some common things

107
00:10:19,120 --> 00:10:25,360
 and that can be exploited to achieve different text processing applications.

108
00:10:25,360 --> 00:10:31,760
 For example if we are dealing with say natural language text we may need to classify the

109
00:10:31,760 --> 00:10:34,960
 particular text into a particular category.

110
00:10:34,960 --> 00:10:39,800
 We may need to group the text in different groups.

111
00:10:39,800 --> 00:10:44,960
 We may need to find out the orientation of the text for example whether this is positive

112
00:10:44,960 --> 00:10:47,460
 or negative and so on and so forth.

113
00:10:47,460 --> 00:10:54,720
 So although they have different tasks they do share the common linguistic characteristics

114
00:10:54,720 --> 00:10:57,240
 that can be exploited in this case.

115
00:10:57,240 --> 00:11:04,600
 Now coming to images for example many images will share the structural similarities.

116
00:11:04,600 --> 00:11:10,560
 For example the shape of an image is an extremely powerful signal which can be used to do many

117
00:11:10,560 --> 00:11:16,800
 downstream tasks for example image classification, image captioning, segmentation and so on and

118
00:11:16,800 --> 00:11:17,800
 so forth.

119
00:11:17,800 --> 00:11:24,200
 Now the same feature for example in case of image the edges can be used to many other

120
00:11:24,200 --> 00:11:25,200
 domains.

121
00:11:25,200 --> 00:11:31,960
 So the question is why can't we exploit the common representation that the tasks share

122
00:11:31,960 --> 00:11:37,040
 so that we can minimize the computational requirements, we can minimize the necessity

123
00:11:37,040 --> 00:11:41,800
 of data and so on and so forth.

124
00:11:41,800 --> 00:11:47,620
 The second and one of the most important sort of the motivations behind transfer learning

125
00:11:47,620 --> 00:11:51,960
 is the annotation cost of data.

126
00:11:51,960 --> 00:11:57,560
 So in order to train a very big deep net you require a lot of data and often the data requires

127
00:11:57,560 --> 00:12:04,440
 annotation and the annotation is not only time consuming at the same time it's expensive

128
00:12:04,440 --> 00:12:12,520
 and at the same time more data that you have there will be a lot of noise and processing

129
00:12:12,520 --> 00:12:18,440
 pre-processing data and cleaning up things will also be extremely challenging.

130
00:12:18,440 --> 00:12:23,520
 So what we do we basically make use of HMAS supervision as available to us and then try

131
00:12:23,520 --> 00:12:29,720
 to modify on top of this sitting on top of this to achieve something.

132
00:12:29,720 --> 00:12:35,560
 The third important point is building large models from scratch is extremely time consuming.

133
00:12:35,560 --> 00:12:38,160
 They are computationally very heavy.

134
00:12:38,160 --> 00:12:43,680
 So not only computationally very heavy they also consume a lot of power so that also is

135
00:12:43,680 --> 00:12:49,680
 kind of damaging for the environment, generates a lot of carbon, footprints and so on so forth.

136
00:12:49,680 --> 00:12:54,680
 So for many different perspective that if you could minimize the computational burden

137
00:12:54,680 --> 00:13:02,320
 for training the deep networks we can do lot of good things and this is one of the major

138
00:13:02,320 --> 00:13:10,280
 objectives of the transfer learning that we are dealing with.

139
00:13:10,280 --> 00:13:14,680
 Now to give a pictorial view of transfer learning.

140
00:13:14,680 --> 00:13:18,280
 Now on the left side you can see the traditional machine learning model.

141
00:13:18,280 --> 00:13:24,360
 So you have different tasks for example task 1, task 2, task 3 and then you have annotated

142
00:13:24,360 --> 00:13:25,360
 data for example.

143
00:13:25,360 --> 00:13:28,160
 These are the annotated data that you have.

144
00:13:28,160 --> 00:13:34,800
 So all the data annotations for different tasks and then there is a model, the different

145
00:13:34,800 --> 00:13:40,520
 learning systems for different data and so on so forth.

146
00:13:40,520 --> 00:13:48,400
 And for each task the machine learning model will learn a new model based on the task and

147
00:13:48,400 --> 00:13:54,320
 the data and they do not share anything between themselves.

148
00:13:54,320 --> 00:13:59,920
 And contrast in transfer learning what happens is that say you have a source task and then

149
00:13:59,920 --> 00:14:04,920
 you have some annotated data in the source task we know and then you have another target

150
00:14:04,920 --> 00:14:09,840
 task for which we do not have annotated data.

151
00:14:09,840 --> 00:14:13,680
 So something that we learn from the source task for example in terms of knowledge in

152
00:14:13,680 --> 00:14:18,920
 terms of particular system and also on so forth can be exploited in this case.

153
00:14:18,920 --> 00:14:25,880
 So we are not going to train the models from scratch every time we encounter a new task.

154
00:14:25,880 --> 00:14:34,560
 What we do we try to figure out often what is the closest possible existing task where

155
00:14:34,560 --> 00:14:37,280
 the model is there.

156
00:14:37,280 --> 00:14:43,080
 Do they share any structural similarity, semantic similarity if the answer is yes.

157
00:14:43,080 --> 00:14:48,960
 We will take that the existing model we will try to modify this in some way or the other

158
00:14:48,960 --> 00:14:53,880
 and then we basically try to apply this on a new task.

159
00:14:53,880 --> 00:14:58,080
 Now of course there are a lot of downsides of it and I will discuss while I talk about

160
00:14:58,080 --> 00:15:06,400
 all of these things later on in few minutes.

161
00:15:06,400 --> 00:15:10,880
 So what are the key questions in transfer learning?

162
00:15:10,880 --> 00:15:15,960
 The first question is what to transfer.

163
00:15:15,960 --> 00:15:22,960
 So it is not very easy that you take something and then go and straight away apply on a new

164
00:15:22,960 --> 00:15:24,720
 task and so on so forth.

165
00:15:24,720 --> 00:15:27,480
 So that may not give you the desired result.

166
00:15:27,480 --> 00:15:33,680
 So the first question that we should be asking ourselves what to transfer.

167
00:15:33,680 --> 00:15:39,440
 So in doing so what we need to do we need to identify which portion of knowledge is source

168
00:15:39,440 --> 00:15:43,840
 specific and what is common between the source and the target.

169
00:15:43,840 --> 00:15:51,720
 So once you identify that then you can target that common representation of the common knowledge

170
00:15:51,720 --> 00:15:59,280
 and modify with respect to the task that we are dealing with and so little bit of like

171
00:15:59,280 --> 00:16:04,760
 changes we can made to the big existing model.

172
00:16:04,760 --> 00:16:09,160
 The second question went to transfer.

173
00:16:09,160 --> 00:16:14,240
 So the ultimate objective is to improve in some way or the other.

174
00:16:14,240 --> 00:16:19,920
 Computationally of course we do not want to degrade the accuracy substantially that something

175
00:16:19,920 --> 00:16:22,600
 has to be kept in mind.

176
00:16:22,600 --> 00:16:28,400
 So our objective would be to utilize transfer learning to improve the target tasks and results

177
00:16:28,400 --> 00:16:32,080
 and we certainly do not want to degrade them.

178
00:16:32,080 --> 00:16:36,600
 So then we have to basically keep in mind when we can do the transfer learning so that

179
00:16:36,600 --> 00:16:38,840
 we can achieve this one.

180
00:16:38,840 --> 00:16:43,600
 Now when I am saying that not degrade them that means I am not saying that you cannot

181
00:16:43,600 --> 00:16:45,680
 degrade them numerically.

182
00:16:45,680 --> 00:16:51,480
 So even if like often in fact we will see that the transfer learning models they do degrade

183
00:16:51,480 --> 00:16:56,640
 performance if you transfer the knowledge compared to if you try to do things from scratch

184
00:16:56,640 --> 00:16:59,640
 using lot of data and big models.

185
00:16:59,640 --> 00:17:04,240
 But the amount of degradation should be minimal.

186
00:17:04,240 --> 00:17:07,440
 If there is substantial amount of degradation then the transfer learning is not going to

187
00:17:07,440 --> 00:17:11,040
 serve the purpose.

188
00:17:11,040 --> 00:17:17,600
 And the third question is the technical question of building these models, training these models

189
00:17:17,600 --> 00:17:20,400
 and all of these things how to transfer.

190
00:17:20,400 --> 00:17:25,680
 So we have to transfer one model to the other tasks and therefore what are the changes that

191
00:17:25,680 --> 00:17:30,720
 you have to make to the existing algorithms and different techniques and how you can train

192
00:17:30,720 --> 00:17:35,120
 and re-train them what are the minimal amount of data that we make use of, computational

193
00:17:35,120 --> 00:17:37,960
 challenges and so on so forth.

194
00:17:37,960 --> 00:17:42,480
 So these three key questions that we need to be very clear about before we try to apply

195
00:17:42,480 --> 00:17:47,780
 transfer learning.

196
00:17:47,780 --> 00:17:57,280
 So these pictures give you the sort of this taxonomy of transfer learning strategies.

197
00:17:57,280 --> 00:18:02,560
 So the lot of things that we know about transfer learning and depending upon the availability

198
00:18:02,560 --> 00:18:08,000
 of data we can categorize a particular thing into a particular category.

199
00:18:08,000 --> 00:18:16,480
 So first of all that we can assume that the label data are available in a target domain.

200
00:18:16,480 --> 00:18:22,920
 So we have a source domain, that's the paradigm we are operating on and we have a target domain.

201
00:18:22,920 --> 00:18:34,680
 And the source domain basically something where we have the original model in this case.

202
00:18:34,680 --> 00:18:42,760
 Then the models comes to the target domain, basically we will try to apply for this one.

203
00:18:42,760 --> 00:18:46,880
 So all basically we are going to do, we are going to this particular model that we have

204
00:18:46,880 --> 00:18:53,360
 in the source domain and in this case if we have some label data available in the target

205
00:18:53,360 --> 00:18:56,560
 domain what we should do.

206
00:18:56,560 --> 00:19:02,640
 So in a setup like this we can apply inductive transfer learning.

207
00:19:02,640 --> 00:19:06,000
 So inductive transfer learning again there are various cases.

208
00:19:06,000 --> 00:19:11,200
 So no label data in a source domain, so what we do we apply something like the source self

209
00:19:11,200 --> 00:19:17,340
 transfer learning, in case we have the label data available in the source domain we can

210
00:19:17,340 --> 00:19:24,720
 apply the kind of learning where the source and the target tasks can be learned simultaneously.

211
00:19:24,720 --> 00:19:27,960
 And all of the things that we do in this case is called multi task learning and I will touch

212
00:19:27,960 --> 00:19:31,840
 upon that particular issue later in this particular lecture.

213
00:19:31,840 --> 00:19:39,960
 In the second scenario the label data are available only in a source domain and the

214
00:19:39,960 --> 00:19:43,760
 no label data available in the target domain.

215
00:19:43,760 --> 00:19:50,280
 In a situation like that what we can do, we can do transductive transfer learning, this

216
00:19:50,280 --> 00:19:53,040
 another branch of transfer learning.

217
00:19:53,040 --> 00:19:57,600
 So the main assumption that we make in this case is that the different domains but single

218
00:19:57,600 --> 00:20:01,760
 task, the same task but the domains are different.

219
00:20:01,760 --> 00:20:08,440
 So what we can do, we can do a thing called the domain adaptation.

220
00:20:08,440 --> 00:20:13,520
 The other assumption that we can make is that the single domain and single task, then in

221
00:20:13,520 --> 00:20:17,080
 that case also we can do the sample selection bias, there could be a lot of issues sample

222
00:20:17,080 --> 00:20:21,800
 selection bias, covariance shift and so on and so forth.

223
00:20:21,800 --> 00:20:29,280
 The third situation in transfer learning the no label data in both source and target domain.

224
00:20:29,280 --> 00:20:33,800
 So in neither of these domain the label data available.

225
00:20:33,800 --> 00:20:38,600
 So in this particular situation we simply do the unsupervised transfer learning, no label

226
00:20:38,600 --> 00:20:43,840
 data is typically used and we can basically transfer one model, the source model to the

227
00:20:43,840 --> 00:20:51,560
 target domain without using any kind of data.

228
00:20:51,560 --> 00:21:00,800
 Now coming to the transfer learning, so again I would like to reiterate that you learn one

229
00:21:00,800 --> 00:21:04,880
 particular task and then transfer to another task.

